    {
      "id": "library/10665-360861/blocks/prose-sec-004",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Background",
      "pageStart": 10,
      "pageEnd": 11,
      "target": "../sections/sec-004-background.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Pre-exposure prophylaxis (PrEP) for HIV prevention \nis the use of antiretroviral drugs by HIV-negative \nindividuals to reduce the risk of acquisition of \nHIV. The World Health Organization (WHO) has \nrecommended multiple PrEP options as part of \ncombination prevention approaches (Box 1). When \nWHO recommended offering TDF-based oral PrEP \nto people at substantial risk for HIV in 2015 (Box 2), \nimplementation of PrEP services was largely limited \nto small-scale studies and projects and high-income \nsettings. Given the limited experience, WHO followed \na cautious “do no harm” principle when deve",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains what PrEP is and recalls that WHO's 2015 recommendation of TDF-based oral PrEP was cautious because experience was limited, whereas services have since been widely simplified, demedicalized and integrated. It states the brief's aim of supporting these trends, accelerated by COVID-19, towards the UN target of fewer than 370 000 new infections by 2025, and summarises WHO PrEP recommendations in a box.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-360861/blocks/prose-sec-005",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Target audience and scope",
      "pageStart": 11,
      "pageEnd": 11,
      "target": "../sections/sec-005-target-audience-and-scope.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "This technical brief aims to support a range of \nstakeholders in planning and implementing PrEP \nservices. It provides guidance on PrEP implementation \nand is meant to supplement and update guidance \npreviously published in the WHO Consolidated HIV \nGuidelines (4) and the WHO PrEP Implementation Tool.3 \nThe guidance in this document relates primarily to \nTDF-based oral PrEP (including TDF in combination with \nemtricitabine [FTC] or lamivudine [3TC]) as most of the \nimplementation experience and evidence available is \nfor oral PrEP. Key principles of the guidance will also \nbe applicable to oth",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes the brief's audience and scope: it supplements WHO consolidated HIV guidelines and the PrEP Implementation Tool, focusing on TDF-based oral PrEP while noting considerations for the dapivirine vaginal ring and long-acting cabotegravir.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-360861/blocks/prose-sec-006",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Guiding principle and methodology",
      "pageStart": 11,
      "pageEnd": 12,
      "target": "../sections/sec-006-guiding-principle-and-methodology.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "This technical brief follows a public health, human \nrights and people-centred approach to provide \nevidence-based guidance on PrEP service delivery. \nSuch an approach puts the people and communities \nwho could benefit from PrEP services at the centre of \nservice delivery, adapting services to their preferences \nand needs while maximizing impact and health system \nefficiency. \nThe development of this brief was led by a core group \nof staff members and consultants of the Testing, \nPrevention and Populations unit of the Global \nHIV, Hepatitis and Sexually Transmitted Infections \nProgrammes of WH",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes the brief's people-centred, rights-based approach and how it was developed by a WHO core group with an expert working group, including management of declarations of interest. It also begins guidance that eligible people assigned male at birth may choose event-driven or daily oral PrEP, with a footnote defining transgender people.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
