    {
      "id": "library/10665-360861/blocks/prose-sec-009",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Starting and stopping oral PrEP",
      "pageStart": 12,
      "pageEnd": 14,
      "target": "../sections/sec-009-starting-and-stopping-oral-prep.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "To reduce the risk of HIV acquisition through sexual \nexposure, cisgender men and trans and gender diverse \npeople assigned male at birth4 who are not taking \nexogenous estradiol-based hormones may be offered \noral daily PrEP or ED-PrEP as options (Table 1 and \nFigure 1). Whether the intention is to use oral daily PrEP \nor ED-PrEP, people eligible for ED-PrEP can start and \nstop PrEP using the same strategy:\n•\t Start PrEP with two doses (loading dose) of TDF-\nbased oral PrEP taken between 2 and 24 hours before \nsexual exposure. Ideally, this loading dose should be \ntaken closer to 24 hours bef",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Sets out how to start and stop oral PrEP: people eligible for event-driven PrEP take a double loading dose 2–24 hours before sex and continue daily until two days after the last exposure. All other groups, including cisgender women and people who inject drugs, take seven daily doses before protection and stop seven days after the last exposure. A figure illustrates the dosing timelines.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-360861/blocks/prose-sec-010",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Rationale",
      "pageStart": 14,
      "pageEnd": 15,
      "target": "../sections/sec-010-rationale.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Data from trials, open label extension studies and \ndemonstration studies have shown that oral PrEP using \nthe ED-PrEP dosing regimen is as effective in preventing \nHIV infection as daily PrEP in cisgender men who have \nsex with men (6–8). As a result, WHO recommended \nthat an ED-PrEP regimen is safe and highly effective in \nreducing risk of HIV acquisition through receptive and/\nor insertive sex between cisgender men and can be \noffered as an alternative to daily dosing for men who \nhave sex with men (6). Importantly, ED-PrEP is effective \nfor all positioning (insertive and/or receptive). It",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains the evidence that event-driven PrEP is as effective as daily PrEP for cisgender men who have sex with men and why it can be extended to all people assigned male at birth not taking estradiol-based hormones, regardless of sexual positioning. It notes insufficient evidence for other groups and compares lead-in and stopping times recommended in other guidelines.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-360861/blocks/prose-sec-011",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "PrEP and hepatitis B and C",
      "pageStart": 15,
      "pageEnd": 15,
      "target": "../sections/sec-011-prep-and-hepatitis-b-and-c.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "B and C\n▶ HBsAg test is non-reactive: WHO recommends HBV \nvaccination for people at risk of acquiring HBV (25).5 \n▶ HBsAg test is reactive: Further assessment for HBV \ntreatment eligibility. \n•\t Eligibility for long-term therapy for HBV infection \nas per WHO guidance (26): \n\t– People with detectable HBsAg and clinical \nevidence of compensated or decompensated \ncirrhosis.\n\t– People older than 30 years with persistently \nabnormal alanine aminotransferase (ALT) levels \nand evidence of high-level HBV replication \n(who do not have clinical evidence of cirrhosis).\n•\t WHO recommends TDF or entecavir",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Gives a flowchart-style summary of hepatitis B management in PrEP services: vaccination for those testing HBsAg-negative and assessment for treatment for those positive. It explains that TDF-based PrEP also suppresses hepatitis B, notes the small risk of relapse after stopping and the need for monitoring.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-360861/blocks/prose-sec-012",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Hepatitis B virus",
      "pageStart": 15,
      "pageEnd": 16,
      "target": "../sections/sec-012-hepatitis-b-virus.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Testing PrEP users for HBsAg once, at or within one to \nthree months of PrEP initiation (or later if not available \naround initiation), is strongly encouraged, particularly \nin highly endemic countries. TDF-based daily or event-\ndriven oral PrEP and the DVR can be safely offered \nto persons with HBV infection. There are specific \nconsiderations regarding offering CAB-LA to people \nwith HBV (Box 3). HBV testing is not a requirement for \nPrEP use. Therefore, lack of HBV testing should not be \na barrier to PrEP initiation or use. Where HBV testing \nis conducted, PrEP can be initiated before resul",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Recommends testing PrEP users once for hepatitis B surface antigen, especially in highly endemic countries, while stressing that lack of testing must not delay PrEP. It notes that oral PrEP and the dapivirine ring are safe for people with hepatitis B or C and repeats the key points on hepatitis testing.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-360861/blocks/prose-sec-013",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Hepatitis C virus",
      "pageStart": 16,
      "pageEnd": 17,
      "target": "../sections/sec-013-hepatitis-c-virus.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "HCV antibody testing is strongly encouraged at or \nwithin one to three months of PrEP initiation (or \nlater if not available around initiation), and every 12 \nmonths thereafter, where PrEP services are provided to \npopulations at high risk of HCV infection. Risks of HCV \nvary across settings, and populations at high risk of \nHCV include, but are not limited to, cisgender men and \ntransgender women who have sex with men, people \nwho use drugs, and people in prisons and other closed \nsettings. TDF-based daily or event-driven oral PrEP \nand the DVR can be safely offered to persons with HCV \ninfec",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Recommends hepatitis C antibody testing at PrEP start and yearly for populations at high risk, while making clear that testing is not a requirement for PrEP. It covers referral for treatment, HCV self-testing and specific considerations for long-acting cabotegravir in people with hepatitis C.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-360861/blocks/prose-sec-014",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Rationale",
      "pageStart": 17,
      "pageEnd": 18,
      "target": "../sections/sec-014-rationale.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "WHO estimates that in 2019, there were 296 million \npeople living with chronic HBV infection and 58 \nmillion people living with HCV infection worldwide \n(31). An estimated 1.1 million deaths in 2019 were due \nto HBV and HCV infection. The Global Health Sector \nStrategy on Viral Hepatitis, approved by the World \nHealth Assembly in 2016, set the goal to eliminate viral \nhepatitis as a major public health threat by 2030 (32). \nHBV and HCV are endemic in many parts of the world \nwhere there is also a high burden of HIV, and many key \nand vulnerable populations are affected by both HIV \nand viral h",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains the rationale for the hepatitis guidance, citing global estimates of hepatitis B and C burden and the 2030 elimination goal. It revises earlier advice by stating that hepatitis B infection does not rule out event-driven PrEP, because clinical relapse is rare, and discusses PrEP choices for pregnant people living with hepatitis B.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
