    {
      "id": "library/10665-360861/blocks/prose-sec-015",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "PrEP and kidney function",
      "pageStart": 18,
      "pageEnd": 20,
      "target": "../sections/sec-015-prep-and-kidney-function.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Impaired kidney function, indicated by an eGFR of \n<60 mL/min per 1.73 m2 or an estimated creatinine \nclearance of <60 mL/min, is a contraindication for the \nuse of TDF-based oral PrEP. Measuring kidney function \nin potential PrEP users at initiation and during PrEP \ncontinuation is suggested for some populations. Table \n2 outlines suggested procedures (applicable to oral \ndaily PrEP or ED-PrEP). Box 4 provides suggestions on \nestimating kidney function. \nWaiting for a kidney function test result should not \ndelay initiation or continuation of oral PrEP, as results \ncan be reviewed at follow-u",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Sets out guidance on kidney function and TDF-based oral PrEP: impaired kidney function is a contraindication, but testing is optional for people under 30 without comorbidities and can be limited for others. It advises that waiting for results should not delay PrEP and that tests be repeated before stopping. Notes cover estimating kidney function, including during pregnancy and gender-affirming hormone use.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-360861/blocks/prose-sec-016",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Rationale",
      "pageStart": 20,
      "pageEnd": 21,
      "target": "../sections/sec-016-rationale.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Multiple systematic reviews and meta-analyses \nof randomized controlled trials (RCTs) found that \nindividuals taking TDF-based oral PrEP have, on \naverage, a higher risk of experiencing kidney-related \nadverse events compared with individuals taking \nplacebos (46–48), but these adverse events tend to \nbe mild, nonprogressive, and reversible after PrEP \ndiscontinuation. Severe kidney-related adverse \nevents are very rare. An analysis of data from 17 \nimplementation projects and two clinical trials from \n15 countries found that less than 1% of over 18 000 \nindividuals screened for PrEP had abnor",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains the rationale for reduced kidney testing, citing meta-analyses showing mostly mild, reversible kidney effects and data from over 18 000 people screened in which abnormal values were rare and increased with age.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-360861/blocks/prose-sec-017",
