    {
      "id": "library/10665-360861/blocks/prose-sec-018",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "HIVST and PrEP",
      "pageStart": 21,
      "pageEnd": 21,
      "target": "../sections/sec-018-hivst-and-prep.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "HIVST could be an important tool to enable countries \nto increase access to PrEP and uptake, persistence \nand effective use. Since the beginning of the COVID-19 \npandemic, WHO has supported HIVST to maintain PrEP \nservices (51), and several countries have integrated \nHIVST into their PrEP programming to initiate and \ncontinue clients on PrEP (see Box 6 and 10 in next \nsection). A literature review found evidence that HIVST \ncan reduce clinic visits for oral PrEP continuation and \nsupport HIV testing between clinic visits, and may \nincrease PrEP use in some groups (52). Experience \nduring COVID",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes how HIV self-testing can support PrEP, drawing on COVID-19 experience and a review showing fewer clinic visits and testing between visits. It outlines three uses—demand generation and linkage, PrEP initiation, and continuation—and notes the need for community engagement and further operational research.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-360861/blocks/prose-sec-019",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "What is HIVST?",
      "pageStart": 21,
      "pageEnd": 23,
      "target": "../sections/sec-019-what-is-hivst.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "In HIVST, people collect their own specimen (generally \noral fluid or fingerprick whole blood) using a simple \nrapid HIV test and then perform the test and interpret \ntheir result, often in private or with someone they \ntrust. WHO has recommended HIVST as an approach \nto HIV testing services since 2016 (4), and the approach \nhas been scaled up globally with nearly 100 countries \nreporting national policies.6 Currently, there are five \nWHO prequalified HIVST products available,7 all of \nwhich have been shown to be highly accurate and \nachieve good performance (49). In field settings, HIVST \nhas",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains what HIV self-testing is, its WHO recommendation since 2016 and the prequalified products available. It discusses using self-testing to start PrEP outside clinics despite limited evidence, addressing concerns about acute infection and drug resistance. Modelling summarised here suggests that self-testing may miss some infections but that drug-resistance prevalence stays similar across scenarios.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-360861/blocks/prose-sec-020",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Rationale",
      "pageStart": 23,
      "pageEnd": 24,
      "target": "../sections/sec-020-rationale.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "A systematic review on HIVST for PrEP initiation and \ncontinuation (52) identified three completed RCTs \nthat evaluated PrEP continuation after initiation in \nhealth care facilities, while no studies evaluated \nHIVST-based PrEP initiation. All studies evaluated \noral PrEP. An RCT in Kenya that evaluated six-month \nPrEP dispensing supported with HIVST between clinic \nvisits found that PrEP clinic visits were halved, while \nPrEP persistence and use were similar to standard-\nof-care three-month PrEP dispensing and clinic-\nbased HIV testing. PrEP use was significantly higher \namong women not in se",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Summarises a systematic review of self-testing for PrEP, including a Kenyan trial in which six-month dispensing with self-testing halved clinic visits without reducing persistence.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
