    {
      "id": "library/10665-360861/blocks/prose-sec-025",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Telehealth for PrEP",
      "pageStart": 29,
      "pageEnd": 31,
      "target": "../sections/sec-025-telehealth-for-prep.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The use of information and telecommunication \ntechnologies to create virtual platforms for delivering \nPrEP services has the potential to remove barriers to \nPrEP uptake and persistence and support effective use \nof PrEP. WHO recommends that online delivery of HIV, \nviral hepatitis and STI services to key populations may \nbe offered as an additional option, while ensuring that \ndata security and confidentiality are protected (28). \nIn many countries, telehealth for PrEP has addressed \naccess challenges during the COVID-19 pandemic.8 A \nsystematic review identified a range of case studies of \nP",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes telehealth platforms for delivering PrEP, which WHO supports as an additional option provided data security and confidentiality are protected. It lists aspects of service delivery that telehealth can support, such as risk assessment, home delivery of self-test kits and prescriptions. Evidence from case studies and ongoing US trials is summarised, with a photo from a Brazilian project.",
