    {
      "id": "library/10665-360861/blocks/prose-sec-026",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "PrEP delivery in pharmacies",
      "pageStart": 31,
      "pageEnd": 32,
      "target": "../sections/sec-026-prep-delivery-in-pharmacies.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Pharmacies can be more accessible, acceptable and \nconvenient for clients than health care facilities. In \nresource-limited settings, individuals commonly first \nseek health care services from pharmacies before \ngoing to clinics (72,73). The WHO Guideline on Self-\nCare Interventions for Health and Well-Being (74) notes \nthat providing PrEP through pharmacies may present \nan opportunity to expand access to PrEP. However, \nfeasibility has only been demonstrated in a limited \nnumber of settings, and the effectiveness and cost–\neffectiveness of this model are unknown (Boxes 11 and \n12). To create",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that pharmacies can be more accessible and convenient than clinics and may expand PrEP access, though effectiveness and cost-effectiveness of pharmacy delivery remain unknown. It sets out what pharmacists need, such as legal authority, testing infrastructure and training, and cites Kenyan experience showing similar uptake and persistence, noting that legal changes are needed for pharmacists to prescribe PrEP.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
