    {
      "id": "library/10665-360861/blocks/prose-sec-027",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Who to deliver PrEP",
      "pageStart": 32,
      "pageEnd": 33,
      "target": "../sections/sec-027-who-to-deliver-prep.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "For HIV treatment, WHO recommends (4) that 1) \ntrained nonphysician clinicians, nurses and midwives \ncan initiate and maintain ART, and 2) trained and \nsupervised community health workers can dispense \nART and lay providers can distribute ART. For PrEP, in \nmost countries a clinician prescribes PrEP, while other \nhealth care providers (for example, lay providers) \nmay deliver other aspects of PrEP services, such as \nassessing whether individuals could benefit from \nPrEP or conducting HIV testing. Task sharing to enable \nPrEP delivery by a range of health workers, including \npeer and community",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Contrasts WHO task-sharing recommendations for ART with PrEP, which in most countries is prescribed by clinicians while lay providers handle other tasks such as risk assessment and HIV testing. It argues that sharing PrEP tasks with peers and community health workers may improve efficiency and acceptability, noting research needs for long-acting cabotegravir and requirements for training, quality assurance, clinical oversight and fair pay.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-360861/blocks/prose-sec-028",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "When and what services to deliver",
      "pageStart": 33,
      "pageEnd": 35,
      "target": "../sections/sec-028-when-and-what-services-to-deliver.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Multi-month dispensing (MMD)\nWHO recommends that people living with HIV \nestablished on ART should be offered three-to-six–\nmonth ART refills, preferably lasting six months (4). \nMMD of ART has been found to be feasible and cost-\nsaving for providers and clients in a range of countries, \nand COVID-19 has accelerated adoption of ART MMD \n(83). For oral PrEP, a one-month supply is commonly \nprovided at initiation, requiring a one-month follow-up \nvisit. This is often appropriate, as it allows PrEP users to \ncheck in with their provider, review laboratory results \nas relevant, discuss issues or c",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Discusses when and what PrEP services to deliver, starting with multi-month dispensing, which is standard for ART and is being adapted for PrEP after an initial one-month visit, with examples from Viet Nam, Zimbabwe and a Kenyan trial of six-month dispensing with HIV self-testing. It then covers integrating PrEP with other services, such as gender-affirming care, harm reduction, antenatal and family planning services, and a status-neutral approach to testing. It ends with a case study from Glasgow on PrEP for people who inject drugs, where staffing intensity limits expansion.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-360861/blocks/prose-sec-029",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Research gaps",
      "pageStart": 35,
      "pageEnd": 36,
      "target": "../sections/sec-029-research-gaps.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "While the feasibility of a range of differentiated PrEP \nservice delivery models has been demonstrated, \nadditional evidence of effectiveness of PrEP-related \noutcomes (uptake, persistence and effective use) \nis needed. Implementation science and quasi-\nexperimental approaches, such as difference-in-\ndifferences methods for pre–post evaluations of \ninterventions, could provide robust evidence on \neffectiveness, particularly since RCTs are often not \nfeasible and do not necessarily provide insight into real-\nworld implementation of services. Moreover, evidence \non feasibility of community-based",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Identifies research gaps for differentiated PrEP delivery, calling for implementation science and quasi-experimental methods to measure effects on uptake, persistence and effective use. It notes that evidence is concentrated among men who have sex with men and young women in a few countries, and that experience with the dapivirine ring and long-acting cabotegravir is still lacking.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
